// EXPECT: [E011]
// ★ AN EMPTY ROW IS STILL A ROW, and it is checked like any other.
//
// `effects` with no names declares the bound ∅ — which is exactly what writing no clause at all
// declares, so the clause buys nothing and the spec lists it as an anti-pattern (plan 7B.5). It is
// not rejected outright, because the thing that makes it wrong is the same thing that makes any
// understated row wrong: the body does more than the bound allows.
//
// Here the body performs IO, so the declared ∅ does not cover it — and the code is [E011], not
// [E130], which is the precedence worth pinning: `io` and `diverge` are DEFAULT-DENIED, so an
// unacknowledged one of those is caught before the row-covers-body comparison ever runs. [E130] is
// what reports an understated `alloc` or `raises`, which are inferred but not denied
// (see effects_alloc_bound_fail.ln).
//
// Either way the answer does not depend on whether the row was written as `effects` or omitted,
// which is the point: they declare the same bound.
extern proc libc_printf(fmt *u8, ...) i32
func noisy(s u8[:0]) effects {
    libc_printf(s.data)
}
func main() i32 { return 0 }
